# SPA routing, rewritten 2026-09-01 to fix a soft 404.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE USED TO BE, and why it changed:
#   /*    /index.html   200
# That single catch-all served the app for EVERY url and returned HTTP 200 for all of
# them. A mistyped or stale link like jeffkurrus.com/anything got "200 OK" plus the
# not-found page, which tells a search engine the page exists and is fine. That is a
# soft 404, and those urls can get indexed.
#
# WHY IT IS DONE THIS WAY, from Cloudflare Pages' own current documentation:
#   1. "Rewrites (other status codes)" are NOT supported in _redirects. Cloudflare
#      lists `/blog/* /blog/404.html 404` explicitly as unsupported. So a 404 STATUS
#      cannot come from this file. It can only come from a top-level 404.html.
#   2. "If your project does not include a top-level 404.html file, Pages assumes that
#      you are deploying a single-page application" and matches all paths to the root.
#      Adding client/public/404.html therefore TURNS OFF that automatic fallback.
#   3. With the fallback off, every real route must be listed here or it will 404.
#      That is what the list below is.
#
# THE RULE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS SITE LATER:
#   Adding a <Route> in client/src/App.tsx is now TWO steps. Add the route there, and
#   add a line here. Miss the second step and the page 404s for anyone who types the
#   url or follows a link into it, while still working when clicked from inside the
#   site. That is a nasty failure to diagnose, so it is written down here.
#
# "/" is deliberately absent: the root serves index.html natively, and adding a rule
# for it risks a loop against Cloudflare's own /index.html -> / redirect.
#
# "/404" is deliberately absent: a request for it should return a real 404.

/books            /index.html   200
/school-visits    /index.html   200
/about            /index.html   200
/photography      /index.html   200
/news             /index.html   200
/contact          /index.html   200
/activity-pack    /index.html   200
